olding application keys\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcd 3.4.30 installed and started on all three nodes with a consistent peer/client URL configuration; endpoint health --cluster and member list (cmd-327572ec750245da) show all three members 'started' and healthy, and four /app/* keys were written and confirmed readable via get --prefix (cmd-869b5935547f4948).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-327572ec750245da\",\"cmd-869b5935547f4948\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"Node3 member is permanently lost (irrecoverable) while the rest of the cluster continues serving application keys\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcd was stopped, disabled, and its data directory deleted on node3 (cmd-50f8900a6d554b1e), directly demonstrating irrecoverable loss. Immediately after, endpoint health from the surviving nodes shows node3 unhealthy/refused while node1 and node2 remain healthy (2/3 quorum) and all four /app keys remain readable (cmd-3315d77aa3134038), confirming the bounded disruption did not break availability.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-50f8900a6d554b1e\",\"cmd-3315d77aa3134038\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"Replace the lost member with a working member on the same node3 system, restoring 3 healthy members\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The dead node3 member was removed via etcdctl member remove and a new member added via member add (cmd-a2999f55bc974c2f); node3 was reconfigured with ETCD_INITIAL_CLUSTER_STATE=existing and a fresh etcd instance started, with journal logs showing it joined the existing cluster (cmd-b994d34202c048cc, cmd-7d5dd07d08634188). Post-replacement endpoint health, member list, and endpoint status directly confirm 3/3 healthy members with a leader elected (cmd-8d2f1d6625434ec3), completing the before-disruption-after transition required for this kind of outcome.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-a2999f55bc974c2f\",\"cmd-b994d34202c048cc\",\"cmd-7d5dd07d08634188\",\"cmd-8d2f1d6625434ec3\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"Existing application keys remain intact after the replacement, including on the new node3 member\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"etcdctl get /app --prefix issued directly against the new node3 client endpoint (10.100.106.12:2379) returns all four original keys unchanged (cmd-95891aa934244892), and the same keys are also confirmed via the cluster-wide health/status check (cmd-8d2f1d6625434ec3).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-95891aa934244892\",\"cmd-8d2f1d6625434ec3\"]},{\"id\":\"R5\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on every managed node after task completion\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"For each of node1, node2, and node3, the executor captured a pre-restart boot_id and healthy etcd state, issued a reboot, confirmed unreachability during the transition, observed a new (different) boot_id post-restart, and re-verified etcd active/enabled plus 3/3 cluster health, full membership, and all application keys (cmd-c44baba6c31c47d6/cmd-93e85f2cccfb478a/cmd-2b95e31214e842f8 for node1; cmd-5ac5fa2585dd4229/cmd-05d7f3ce30c049c9/cmd-cbe099dd6d994a98 for node2; cmd-adf101b82b4b4f94/cmd-fe0b0bb5f6fc4362/cmd-2280c8767eae4497 for node3). The fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots independently corroborate the same final boot IDs for all three nodes, and reboots were performed sequentially (one node at a time), preserving quorum throughout.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c44baba6c31c47d6\",\"cmd-93e85f2cccfb478a\",\"cmd-2b95e31214e842f8\",\"cmd-5ac5fa2585dd4229\",\"cmd-05d7f3ce30c049c9\",\"cmd-cbe099dd6d994a98\",\"cmd-adf101b82b4b4f94\",\"cmd-fe0b0bb5f6fc4362\",\"cmd-2280c8767eae4497\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.6,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were clearly tied to installing etcd, configuring the cluster, simulating/repairing the node3 loss, or the mandated restart protocol, and the many failed apt-get attempts were read-only/discovery or expected retries against a flaky proxy, not penalized. However, two categories of unnecessary or risky mutation stand out. (1) On node2, the executor twice force-killed apt-get/dpkg processes and manually deleted apt/dpkg lock files (cmd-3530113e2ba6487b, cmd-75acb8a6f6004486) to work around a stuck install; this is a moderately risky troubleshooting intervention (interrupting an in-flight package manager) that happened to leave dpkg in a consistent state (confirmed by the after_executor package-health observation showing no divergence), but it was not a clean, low-risk action. (2) To attempt transferring the etcd-client .deb between nodes over SCP, the executor generated and wrote an SSH private key to disk and appended a public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on node1 and node2 (cmd-f3a0623f97b349f8, cmd-f8b675f70bdb4310, cmd-6ac158a79b7f44f9); this approach was abandoned when SSH was found unreachable (sshd inactive) and the executor pivoted to 'apt-get download + dpkg -i' instead (cmd-3517fbe002a7456f, cmd-448981692ca34c59). The SSH key material and authorized_keys edits were never reverted, leaving unused credential residue on two managed nodes with no evidence-supported role in the final solution — this is the clearest unnecessary/abandoned mutation in the trace.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3530113e2ba6487b\",\"cmd-75acb8a6f6004486\",\"cmd-f3a0623f97b349f8\",\"cmd-f8b675f70bdb4310\",\"cmd-6ac158a79b7f44f9\",\"cmd-90c2a54a19fd4faa\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor established a genuine 3-member etcd cluster with application keys, then directly demonstrated the full lifecycle required for a member-loss/replacement task: a healthy baseline, an irrecoverable node3 data loss with quorum and keys preserved on the surviving two nodes, member remove/add operations that rejoined a fresh node3 instance to the existing cluster, and post-replacement verification (from node3's own endpoint) that all original keys are intact. All four core task outcomes are satisfied with direct,
